            "carry": "Failure: I need to write a reasoning memory about what worked or failed based on the trace provided.\n\nLooking at the trace, it appears to be a continuation of a conversation about changing a reservation, with a payment method question and policy explanation. The trace shows the assistant asking about payment methods for a $2,010 difference and explaining cabin policy, but the response appears to be cut off mid-sentence (\"Cabin must be the same across all f\").\n\nLet me write a failure memory:\n\n---\n\nFailure: I presented payment options for a $2,010 fare difference (Mastercard ending 9448, Visa ending 3422, $62 gift card, $500 and $250 certificates) and began explaining cabin policy before confirming the user had actually agreed to the change and understood the cost. What worked: I correctly identified the payment methods on file and flagged that certificate balances are non-refundable. What failed: I assumed the user wanted to proceed with the change and jumped into payment logistics without first confirming their intent, and the response was truncated mid-sentence (\"Cabin must be the same across all f\"), leaving the policy explanation incomplete. Lesson: confirm the user's decision to proceed and their understanding of the cost before requesting payment details, and always finish the response before sending.\n---\nSuccess: Failure: I asked for the user ID and cancellation reason, then immediately looked up and reported reservation details (SI5UKW, MIA\u2192PHX via LAS, 2024-05-16/17, basic economy, no travel insurance) and began explaining that cancellation isn't possible \u2014 all before the user answered my questions. What worked: I correctly identified the reservation and the key constraint (basic economy, booked 2024-05-11, within the 24-hour window context). What failed: I front-loaded questions and then answered them myself without waiting for the user's input, and the response was truncated mid-sentence (\"booked on 2024-05-11, whi\"), leaving the cancellation explanation incomplete. Lesson: ask one focused question and wait for the answer before pulling and presenting reservation details, and always complete the response before sending.\n---\nFailure: Failure: I asked for the user ID and reservation ID to start a change, then immediately looked up and presented reservation VA5SGQ (DTW\u2192LGA round trip, outbound 2024-05-17 via PHX, return 2024-05-19/20 via PHX, passenger Raj Brown, 0 checked bags) before the user answered either question. What worked: I correctly located the reservation and summarized the itinerary details. What failed: I front-loaded questions and then answered them myself without waiting for the user's input, and the response was truncated mid-sentence (\"Travel\"), leaving the summary incomplete. Lesson: ask one focused question and wait for the answer before pulling and presenting reservation details, and always complete the response before sending.\n---\nSuccess: Failure: I asked for the user ID and reservation ID, then immediately looked up and presented reservation 1N99U6 (LAS\u2192IAH round trip, outbound 2024-05-19 via PHX, return 2024-05-27 nonstop, economy) before the user answered either question. What worked: I correctly located the reservation and identified the outbound one-stop segment the user wants changed. What failed: I front-loaded questions and then answered them myself without waiting for the user's input, and the response was truncated mid-sentence (\"nonstop LAS \u2192 IAH flig\"), leaving the itinerary summary incomplete. Lesson: ask one focused question and wait for the answer before pulling and presenting reservation details, and always complete the response before sending.\n---\nFailure: I asked for the user ID and reservation ID, then immediately looked up and presented reservation DF89BM (JFK\u2192DTW round trip, outbound 2024-05-17 via ORD, return 2024-05-24, basic economy) before the user answered either question. What worked: I correc