tion, then stop and wait for the user's reply before pulling further details.\n---\nSuccess: Success: I verified the account and pulled the order details, then presented the water bottle variants as a compact list with short summary lines (size, material, color, price) rather than a long itemized breakdown \u2014 that avoided truncation and the response finished cleanly. Lesson: when presenting multiple product variants, keep each entry to a single short line (key options + price), cap the list or summarize the count, and end with one focused question \u2014 then stop and wait for the user's reply before pulling further details.\n---\nFailure: I asked for the email, then immediately offered name/zip as an alternative in the same turn, and when the user gave name/zip I proceeded to verify \u2014 but the verification failed and I ended up asking for both a correction and the email again, producing a muddled, truncated response. Lesson: offer only one verification path per turn and stop; don't stack the primary request, the fallback, and a verification attempt into a single response \u2014 pick one, finish it cleanly, and wait for the user's reply before trying to verify.\n---\nSuccess: I verified the account and pulled up the order details, then presented the three orders as a compact summary rather than a long itemized listing \u2014 that avoided truncation and the response finished cleanly. Lesson: when presenting multiple orders, keep each entry to a short summary line (order ID, item, key specs, price), cap or summarize the count, and end with one focused question \u2014 then stop and wait for the user's reply before pulling further details.\n---\nSuccess: I verified the account and pulled up the order details, then presented the two delivered orders as a compact summary (item, key specs, price) rather than a long itemized variant listing \u2014 that avoided truncation and the response finished cleanly. Lesson: when presenting multiple orders or product variants, 