annot be done \u2014 and asking for identity verification upfront \u2014 keeps the interaction honest and sets correct expectations.\n---\nSuccess: I was helping a customer who wanted to change an item in their order, and they had asked about swapping a luggage set for a coat \u2014 a product-type change, not an option change. The key lesson: before diving into order lookups or promising anything, I should first clarify the policy boundary (only same-product option changes are allowed, not product-type swaps) and verify identity upfront. Being transparent about what can and cannot be done, and setting correct expectations early, keeps the interaction honest and prevents wasted effort on a change that policy won't permit.\n---\nSuccess: I was helping a customer who wanted to change an item in their order, and I attempted identity verification using name and zip code. The lookup failed \u2014 no account matched \"Noah Ito\" with zip code 98178. The key lesson: when name-plus-zip verification fails, don't stall or guess; immediately offer the alternative verification path (email address) and ask the customer to double-check the spelling and zip. Being flexible about verification methods and transparent about the failed lookup keeps the interaction moving without compromising account security.\n---\nSuccess: I was helping a customer who wanted to change an item in their order, and I attempted identity verification using name and zip code. The lookup failed \u2014 no account matched \"Noah Ito\" with zip code 98178. The key lesson: when name-plus-zip verification fails, don't stall or guess; immediately offer the alternative verification path (email address) and ask the customer to double-check the spelling and zip. Being flexible about verification methods and transparent about the failed lookup keeps the interaction moving without compromising account security.",
