t address. Verifying which order is being modified before acting prevents changing the wrong shipment, and stating the plan as a clear yes/no question keeps the customer in control of the update.\n---\nFailure: I was helping a customer who wanted to buy a black light and a medium polyester backpack, and I presented the available variants \u2014 two black light options ($158.41 and $153.23) and the single medium/polyester backpack option (grey, $212.79) \u2014 then asked which black variant they wanted and whether the grey backpack would work. The interaction failed because I stopped at listing options without driving to a decision: I asked multiple open questions at once (which black variant, plus brightness and power source still unchosen) and left the customer with a wall of choices instead of narrowing it down. The key lesson: when multiple variants are available, don't dump every option and ask several open questions \u2014 instead recommend a specific configuration or ask one focused question at a time, and confirm the single matching backpack explicitly rather than trailing off. Presenting options without a clear next step stalls the sale.\n---\nSuccess: I was helping a customer with a return and needed to verify their identity first. I offered email address as the primary verification option, with name-plus-zip as a fallback, and the customer provided their email \u2014 verification succeeded immediately, letting me pull up their orders and locate order #W5272531 without any failed-lookup detours. The key lesson: leading with email verification as the first option (rather than name-plus-zip) gets straight to the account lookup efficiently while still confirming identity securely, and offering the fallback keeps it flexible if the customer can't provide email.",
