            "carry": "Success: I'm going to stop here rather than continue, because the trace shows the same failure pattern the memory documents \u2014 and it's happening again in real time.\n\n**What the trace shows:**\n- I opened correctly with a verification gate (good \u2014 that part worked).\n- But then I immediately said *\"I'll look up your account with that email\"* and *\"I couldn't find an acc[ount]\"* \u2014 meaning I attempted an order/account lookup **before** the user provided any verification data, and I started reporting lookup results back to them.\n\n**Why this fails:**\nThe memory's core lesson is: *\"No order lookup... until that gate is passed. The first move on any order-specific request is always the verification ask \u2014 never the order action.\"* I asked for verification, but then performed the lookup anyway and disclosed a result (\"couldn't find an account\"). That's the exact leak the memory warns against \u2014 it confirms account-existence information to an unverified requester.\n\n**What worked:**\n- The verification ask itself was clean, specific, and offered the two acceptable options (email OR full name + zip code).\n- I did not quote prices, stock, payment methods, or order details.\n\n**What failed:**\n- I ran a lookup and reported its outcome before receiving/confirming verification data.\n- The message trails off mid-lookup, which means the action was already in motion.\n\n**Correct next move:**\nSend a clean restart that (1) asks for the verification data, (2) explicitly states I will *not* look anything up until it's provided and confirmed, and (3) makes no reference to any lookup result. Something like:\n\n> \"Happy to help with the exchange. To protect your account, I can't look up or discuss any order details until I verify you. Please send **either** the email on the order **or** your full name + billing zip code. Once you provide it, I'll confirm it and then proceed \u2014 I won't access the account before that.\"\n\nI'm flagging this instead of continuing because proceeding would repeat the documented security failure. Want me to finalize that restart message?\n---\nFailure: **Memory: Order-specific requests require verification before any account action \u2014 and \"asking\" is not \"gating.\"**\n\n**What failed:** I asked for verification, then immediately performed an order/account lookup and disclosed the result (\"couldn't find an account\") before the user provided any verification data. The trace confirms the same pattern recurring: the verification ask was issued, but the lookup ran anyway, and order details (Order #W1304208, item, price $253.89, refund info) were surfaced in the response. Asking for verification while simultaneously acting on the account is not a gate \u2014 it's a leak with a polite preamble.\n\n**Root cause:** I treated the verification ask as a box to check rather than a hard stop. The lookup was already \"in motion\" before confirmation, so the gate never actually blocked anything. The failure isn't the wording of the ask \u2014 it's that no state change occurred between \"ask\" and \"act.\"\n\n**What worked:** The verification ask itself was clean and specific (email OR full name + zip code), and I avoided quoting stock/payment methods. But that's cosmetic if the lookup proceeds regardless.\n\n**Correct behavior:** The first move on any order-specific request is the verification ask \u2014 and then *stop*. No lookup, no account-existence confirmation, no order details, no trailing \"I couldn't find...\" until verification data is received and confirmed. If a lookup was already attempted, do not reference its result; restart cleanly with the verification request and an explicit statement that no account access will occur until verification is confirmed.\n\n**Rule to carry forward:** A verification gate only counts if the action is genuinely blocked pending input. If the lookup runs in the same turn as the ask, the gate failed \u2014 regardless of how the ask was phrased.\n---\nSuccess: **Memory: Verification gates must block the action, not just precede it.**\n\n**What failed:** I stated I needed to verify identity before looking up order details, then in the same turn announced \"I'll verify your identity now\" and proceeded \u2014 the gate was cosmetic. The ask and the action occupied the same turn, so nothing was actually blocked. This is the same pattern as before: a polite preamble followed by the very lookup it was supposed to prevent.\n\n**Root cause:** I treated the verification request as a conversational step rather than a hard state boundary. The turn ended with an action commitment (\"I'll verify your identity now\") instead of a stop-and-wait. No state change separated \"ask\" from \"act,\" so the gate never gated anything.\n\n**What worked:** The verification ask itself was specific and offered clear alternatives (email OR full name + zip). That's the right shape \u2014 but shape doesn't matter if the action proceeds anyway.\n\n**Correct behavior:** On any order-specific request, the first and only move is the verification ask, then stop. End the turn with the request and an explicit statement that no lookup will occur until verification data is received. Do not announce that verification is \"starting,\" do not preview what will be looked up, and do not reference any lookup result in the same turn.\n\n**Rule to carry forward:** A gate counts only if the action is genuinely blocked pending user input. If the turn ends with the action still in motion, the gate failed \u2014 regardless of how clean the ask was.",
