            "carry": "Success: **Memory: A verification gate that leaks the protected data in the same turn is not a gate.**\n\n**What failed:** I asked the user to confirm which order to update and provide a new address \u2014 but in the same turn I had already performed the order lookup and disclosed order numbers, item counts, totals, statuses, and full shipping addresses. The \"gate\" (asking for confirmation) came *after* the sensitive data was already on screen. The user never had to verify anything to receive the information the gate was supposed to protect.\n\n**Root cause:** I conflated \"asking a clarifying question\" with \"gating access.\" The lookup ran first, the disclosure happened, and only then did I append a question. The turn ended with data already delivered \u2014 so nothing was blocked. This is the same failure mode as before, just inverted: previously the ask preceded the action in the same turn; here the action preceded the ask in the same turn. Either ordering fails, because the ask and the action share a turn.\n\n**What worked:** The clarifying question itself was well-formed \u2014 it named the ambiguity (two orders, both on Elm Avenue), surfaced the default address as context, and asked for a specific new address. That's the right shape for a *post-verification* clarification. But it cannot substitute for verification.\n\n**Correct behavior:** On any order-specific request, the first and only move is the verification ask, then stop. Do not look up orders, do not summarize them, do not disclose order numbers, totals, statuses, or addresses \u2014 not even partially, not even as \"context\" for the question. End the turn with the verification request and an explicit statement that no account or order access will occur until verification is confirmed. Clarifying questions about *which* order come only after verification succeeds.\n\n**Rule to carry forward:** A gate counts only if the protected action is genuinely blocked pending user input \u2014 and \"blocked\" means no protected data appears in the gating turn, in any form, before or after the ask. If the turn contains both the ask and the disclosure, the gate failed regardless of ordering.\n---\nFailure: # Reasoning Memory: Failed Gate \u2014 Post-Hoc Clarification After Disclosure\n\n## What I attempted\nThe user asked to update the shipping address on an order. I recognized there was ambiguity (two orders, both on Elm Avenue) and formulated a clarifying question asking which order to update and requesting the new address. The question itself was well-formed: it named the ambiguity, surfaced the default address as context, and asked for a specific new address.\n\n## What failed\nThe gate was cosmetic. In the same turn, *before* appending the clarifying question, I had already performed the order lookup and disclosed order numbers (#W1092119, #W1603792), item counts, totals, statuses, and full shipping addresses (including \"592 Elm Avenue, Suite 978, Houston, TX 77242\"). The user received all the sensitive data the gate was supposed to protect *without verifying anything*. The ask came after the disclosure \u2014 so nothing was blocked.\n\n## Root cause\nI conflated \"asking a clarifying question\" with \"gating access.\" The lookup ran first, disclosure happened, and only then did I append a question. The turn ended with data already delivered. This is the same failure mode as a prior incident, just inverted: previously the ask preceded the action in the same turn; here the action preceded the ask. **Either ordering fails, because the ask and the action share a turn.**\n\n## What worked\nThe clarifying question's *shape* was correct \u2014 it identified the specific ambiguity, provided minimal context, and requested a concrete input. That shape is appropriate for a *post-verification* clarification. It cannot substitute for verification.\n\n## Correct behavior\nOn any order-specific request, the first and only move is the verification ask, then stop. Do not look up orders. Do not summarize them. Do not disclose order numbers, totals, statuses, or addresses \u2014 not even partially, not even as \"context\" for the question. End the turn with the verification request and an explicit statement that no account or order access will occur until verification is confirmed. Clarifying questions about *which* order come only after verification succeeds.\n\n## Rule to carry forward\nA gate counts only if the protected action is genuinely blocked pending user input \u2014 and \"blocked\" means **no protected data appears in the gating turn, in any form, before or after the ask**. If a turn contains both the ask and the disclosure, the gate failed regardless of ordering.",
