            "carry": "Failure: ## What failed\nThe gate was cosmetic. In the same turn, *before* appending the clarifying question, I had already performed the order lookup and disclosed order numbers (#W1092119, #W1603792), item counts, totals, statuses, and full shipping addresses (including \"592 Elm Avenue, Suite 978, Houston, TX 77242\"). The user received all the sensitive data the gate was supposed to protect *without verifying anything*. The ask came after the disclosure \u2014 so nothing was blocked.\n\n## Root cause\nI conflated \"asking a clarifying question\" with \"gating access.\" The lookup ran first, disclosure happened, and only then did I append a question. The turn ended with data already delivered. This is the same failure mode as a prior incident, just inverted: previously the ask preceded the action in the same turn; here the action preceded the ask. **Either ordering fails, because the ask and the action share a turn.**\n\n## What worked\nThe clarifying question's *shape* was correct \u2014 it identified the specific ambiguity, provided minimal context, and requested a concrete input. That shape is appropriate for a *post-verification* clarification. It cannot substitute for verification.\n\n## Correct behavior\nOn any order-specific request, the first and only move is the verification ask, then stop. Do not look up orders. Do not summarize them. Do not disclose order numbers, totals, statuses, or addresses \u2014 not even partially, not even as \"context\" for the question. End the turn with the verification request and an explicit statement that no account or order access will occur until verification is confirmed. Clarifying questions about *which* order come only after verification succeeds.\n\n## Rule to carry forward\nA gate counts only if the protected action is genuinely blocked pending user input \u2014 and \"blocked\" means **no protected data appears in the gating turn, in any form, before or after the ask**. If a turn contains both the ask and the disclosure, the gate failed regardless of ordering.\n---\nFailure: ## What failed\nI disclosed a prior order number (#W3964602) and confirmed a New York shipping address was on file *before* the user had verified their identity or provided the new address details. The turn ended with order-specific information already delivered \u2014 the verification/collection ask came bundled with a partial disclosure, so the gate was cosmetic.\n\n## Root cause\nSame failure mode as before: I treated \"asking for the details needed to proceed\" as if it were a gate, while simultaneously surfacing account-specific data as \"context\" for the request. The disclosure and the ask shared a single turn, so nothing was actually blocked. Mentioning the prior order and its shipping region was unnecessary to the request and leaked protected information.\n\n## What worked\nThe request itself was well-scoped \u2014 it enumerated exactly which fields were needed (address lines, city, state, zip, country) and committed to confirming before applying the change. That structure is correct *after* verification, but it cannot substitute for verification.\n\n## Correct behavior\nOn any order- or account-specific request, the first and only move is the verification ask, then stop. Do not reference prior orders, order numbers, shipping regions, or any account details \u2014 not even as \"context.\" End the turn with the verification request and an explicit statement that no account or order access will occur until verification is confirmed. Only after verification succeeds do I collect the new address details and confirm before applying.\n\n## Rule to carry forward\nA gate counts only if no protected data appears in the gating turn, in any form, before or after the ask. Referencing a prior order number or confirming an address \"on file\" is a disclosure \u2014 if it appears alongside the ask, the gate failed.",
