time: the first turn asked only for identity-verification inputs (email, or name + zip) and explicitly stated that no account or order access would occur until verification was confirmed. No order numbers, addresses, or account details appeared in that gating turn \u2014 the gate was real, not cosmetic.\n\n## What failed\nNothing in the gating turn, but the trace shows the post-verification flow beginning to enumerate orders (\"look up your orders to find the pending ones,\" \"check the status of each\") without yet confirming which order the user wanted changed or restating the change before acting. The risk is scope creep: once verified, it's tempting to dump all pending orders rather than confirm the specific one and the intended change first.\n\n## Root cause\nVerification succeeded, so the pressure shifted to \"now do everything at once.\" The correct discipline after a gate passes is to narrow to the specific order and confirm the intended action before disclosing or modifying anything \u2014 not to enumerate the whole account.\n\n## Rule to carry forward\nA clean gate is necessary but not sufficient. After verification, confirm the specific order and the exact change before disclosing order details or applying anything. Verification unlocks access; it does not license bulk disclosure.",
