            "carry": "# Shared Skill Library\n\n## File System Operations\n\n- **Searching**: When searching for files by name, use `find` with explicit scope and report all matches. If no exact matches exist, state that clearly before offering partial/fuzzy alternatives.\n- **Listing by modification time**: Use `find <scope> -name \"<pattern>\" -exec ls -lt {} +` to sort matches by mtime. If the pattern yields nothing, do not fabricate results\u2014report the empty result set.\n- **Text file scanning**: Prefer `grep -I` (skip binary) over relying on `file` being installed. Account for zero-length files explicitly\u2014decide whether they belong in the result set and state the choice.\n- **Duplicate Detection**: When finding duplicate filenames, clarify whether matching is case-sensitive or case-insensitive, and whether comparison is by name only or by content. Enumerate all matches before reporting; state the exact paths found.\n\n## Code Editing\n\n- Make minimal, targeted edits rather than wholesale rewrites.\n- Preserve existing style, indentation, and conventions.\n- Re-read edited regions to verify correctness.\n\n## Testing & Validation\n\n- Run relevant tests after changes. Report failures with the exact command and output.\n- Verify environment assumptions (paths, tools, versions) before relying on them.\n- Probe for required tools (e.g., `zip`, `tar`, `gzip`, `file`) before choosing an approach; fall back to available alternatives (e.g., Python `zipfile`/`tarfile`, `grep -I`) when a tool is missing, and state the substitution.\n\n## Version Control\n\n- Use `git` for diffs, status, and commits.\n- Never force-push or rewrite history without explicit instruction.\n\n## Communication\n\n- **Clarity**: State assumptions explicitly. Ask when requirements are ambiguous rather than guessing.\n- **Conciseness**: Return only what was requested. Avoid filler and restating the prompt.\n- **Error Reporting**: When something fails, include the command, error text, and